    "lunar": "month/paksha/tithi are given in Purnimanta naming (Krishna-paksha months are one later than Amanta); the engine converts. Shukla-paksha rules are identical in both systems.",
    "timing": "udaya = tithi at sunrise (first day unless prefer=last; kshaya -> begin day); aparahna = sunrise + 0.7 daylight; madhyahna = sunrise + 0.5 daylight; pradosh = sunset + night/10 (window sunset -> sunset + night/5), later day preferred for Diwali/Dhanteras/Holika Dahan (Dharmasindhu 'dinadvaye para'); nishita = middle of the night +-24 min; moonrise = tithi at the Vedic-day moonrise; purvahna = sunrise + 0.25 daylight (Vasant Panchami); minMuhurtas = N: an udaya tithi that runs fewer than N muhurtas (48 min) after its only sunrise moves back to the day it began (Akshaya Tritiya, N = 3, Nirnaya Sindhu tri-muhurta rule); ekadashiVrata: an Ekadashi whose following Dwadashi touches no sunrise (kshaya) is kept on the day the Ekadashi began so the parana stays inside the Dwadashi, and two Ekadashi sunrises take the later day (A04 vs Drik Panchang 2026-2028).",
    "solar": "sign = sidereal sign the Sun enters (0 = Mesha); dayRule sunset = the civil day of the sankranti, or the next day when the sankranti is after sunset; sunrise = the next day when the sankranti is after sunrise (Malayalam Vishu); same-day = the civil date of the sankranti (Baisakhi).",
    "nakshatra": "the day in the solar month whose DAYLIGHT the nakshatra covers most (the passage nearest the full moon when the Moon passes twice); a day whose sunrise lies inside the nakshatra wins only when it has more daylight under it; no daylight overlap at all -> the begin day.",
    "adhik": "an Adhik month never carries a festival; the nija month of the same name does.",
